## Program.cs (stdio transport)

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace); // stderr only — stdout is for JSON-RPC

builder.Services.AddHttpClient();  // registers IHttpClientFactory + HttpClient via DI

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

## Tool class wrapping a REST API

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Fetches a resource by its unique identifier.")]
    public async Task<string> GetResource(
        [Description("The unique resource identifier")] string id,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching resource {Id}", id);
        var response = await httpClient.GetAsync($"/api/resources/{id}", cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Lists all available resources, optionally filtered by category.")]
    public async Task<string> ListResources(
        [Description("Optional category filter")] string? category = null,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var url = category is not null ? $"/api/resources?category={category}" : "/api/resources";
        return await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url, cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool(ReadOnly = false), Description("Creates a new resource.")]
    public async Task<string> CreateResource(
        [Description("Name of the resource to create")] string name,
        [Description("Resource description")] string description,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var payload = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { name, description });
        var content = new StringContent(payload, System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");
        var response = await httpClient.PostAsync("/api/resources", content, cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

## How DI works for MCP tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

| Style | Class type | How services arrive |
|-------|-----------|---------------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static class (shown above) | Services injected via primary constructor. The SDK creates an instance per call, resolving `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, etc. from the DI container. |
| **Method parameter injection** | Static class | DI services appear as method parameters alongside tool parameters. The SDK distinguishes them automatically — DI services are **not** exposed in the tool schema. |

**Key rules:**
- `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<T>`, and any DI-registered service are **auto-injected** and hidden from the LLM's tool schema
- Only parameters with `[Description]` are visible to the LLM
- `AddHttpClient()` in Program.cs registers both `IHttpClientFactory` and `HttpClient` — either can be injected
- `ILogger<T>` is registered by default, no extra setup needed
- Logging **must** go to stderr (via `LogToStandardErrorThreshold`) — stdout is reserved for the JSON-RPC protocol on stdio transport
